                <p class="snippet" >
                    The scope and interpretation of the amended Rule 20 summary judgment motion are addressed in the companion appeal, <b>Hryniak v. Mauldin</b>, 2014 SCC 7, [2014] 1 S.C.R. 87. Summary judgment may not be granted under Rule 20 where there is a genuine issue requiring a trial. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;Applied: <b>Hryniak v. Mauldin</b>, 2014 SCC 7, [2014] 1 S.C.R. 87; referred to: Derry v. Peek (1889), 14 App. Cas. 337; Parna v. G. &amp; S. Properties Ltd., [1971] S.C.R. 306; Snell v. Farrell, [1990] 2 S.C.R. 311; Angers v. Mutual Reserve Fund Life Assn. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[1] Karakatsanis J. — Like its companion, <b>Hryniak v. Mauldin</b>, 2014 SCC 7, [2014] 1 S.C.R. 87 (Mauldin), this appeal concerns the interpretation and application of Ontario’s new summary judgment rules. 
